{"candidates": [{"conjecture": "Place 8 points at the vertices of a regular octagon inscribed in the unit square (touching the midpoints of the sides) plus 5 points: the center and 4 points at (a,a), (1-a,a), (a,1-a), (1-a,1-a) for a≈0.2. The dihedral D4 symmetry reduces the search to one parameter a, and the optimal a maximizes the minimum among triangles formed by adjacent octagon vertices and the inner shell points. The minimum triangle area is approximately 0.0185.", "probability": 0.085}, {"conjecture": "Place 4 points at the corners of the unit square, 4 points at the midpoints of the sides, and 5 points on the diagonals at positions (d,d), (1-d,d), (d,1-d), (1-d,1-d), and (0.5,0.5) with d≈0.25. The D4 symmetry constrains all triangles to have area at least 0.0156, achieved by triangles of three collinear-near points on the diagonals.", "probability": 0.072}, {"conjecture": "Place 12 points on two concentric squares: 4 at corners (0,0),(1,0),(1,1),(0,1) and 8 at positions (a,0),(1,a),(1,1-a),(a,1),(1-a,0),(0,1-a),(0,a),(1-a,1-a) with a≈0.3, plus the center (0.5,0.5). The D4 symmetry reduces the search to parameter a, and the minimum triangle area is approximately 0.02.", "probability": 0.079}, {"conjecture": "Place 8 points on a circle of radius r centered at (0.5,0.5) at 45-degree intervals, plus 4 points at the corners (0,0),(1,0),(1,1),(0,1), plus the center. With r chosen so the circle passes through points like (0.5±r/√2, 0.5±r/√2), the D4 symmetry constrains the minimum triangle area to approximately 0.017, limited by triangles of nearby circle points with the center.", "probability": 0.068}, {"conjecture": "Place 4 points at corners, 4 at (a,0),(1,a),(1,1-a),(a,1), 4 at (b,0),(1,b),(1,1-b),(b,1) with b=1-a, a≈0.28, and the center (0.5,0.5). The D4 symmetry reduces to one parameter, and the minimum triangle area is approximately 0.019, achieved by adjacent points on the same side.", "probability": 0.075}, {"conjecture": "Place 9 points on a 3×3 grid with spacing 0.5 (i.e., at coordinates 0, 0.5, 1 in both x and y), plus 4 points at (0.25,0.25),(0.75,0.25),(0.25,0.75),(0.75,0.75). The D4 symmetry is already built in, and the minimum triangle area is 0.0625/4 = 0.015625, limited by triples of adjacent grid-internal points.", "probability": 0.082}, {"conjecture": "Place 12 points on the boundary at (i/12, 0), (1, i/12), (1-i/12, 1), (0, 1-i/12) for i=0,1,2,3 plus the center (0.5,0.5). The C4 symmetry reduces the search to the spacing, and with equal spacing the minimum triangle area is approximately 0.0208, achieved by three consecutive boundary points.", "probability": 0.065}, {"conjecture":": "Place 4 points at the corners, 8 points at (a,a), (1-a,a), (a,1-a), (1-a,1-a), (0.5,a), (1-a,0.5), (0.5,1-a), (a,0.5) with a≈0.22, and the center (0.5,0.5). The D4 symmetry reduces the search to one parameter, and the minimum triangle area is approximately 0.016, achieved by triangles of three nearby inner points.", "probability": 0.061}, {"conjecture": "Place 4 points at the corners, 4 at (0.5,0), (1,0.5), (0.5,1), (0,0.5), 4 at (a,0), (1,a), (1-a,1), (0,1-a) with a≈0.25, and the center. The D4 symmetry constrains all triangles to have area at least 0.0156, with the minimum achieved by adjacent boundary points with the center.", "probability": 0.058}, {"conjecture": "Place 12 points on three concentric squares: 4 corners, 4 at (0.25,0.25),(0.75,0.25),(0.25,0.75),(0.75,0.75), and 4 at (0.5,0.25),(0.75,0.5),(0.5,0.75),(0.25,0.5), plus the center. The D4 symmetry is fixed, and the minimum triangle area is approximately 0.0156, limited by three nearby inner square points.", "probability": 0.070}]}
